\relax 
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{1. two factors}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Both bold subgraphs have degree two at every vertex. Only the lower one is connected. These are the same 24-vertex graph, dual to the running triangulation.}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{2. dual}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The running Eulerian triangulation has 14 vertices and 24 faces. Grey interiors denote black faces; the unbounded face is also black. Hollow vertices are the three roots. Vertex numbers remain fixed throughout.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{3. cycle trees}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Every edge within either vertex class is bold. The duals of all remaining edges form the single cycle below. Circle and square classes remain distinguishable without colour.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{4. state}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces A blue disk marks the corner $s(t)$ inside each bounded black triangle. Every nonroot receives exactly one mark; roots receive none. This state has a disjoint partner and a forest $Q_s$.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{5. pair D}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The two states choose different corners in every black face. Their directed edges split the nonroots into three cycles. The lower panel removes corner marks to expose those cycles.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{6. Q}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Each dashed edge lies opposite the second-state mark. The upper state has a cycle; the lower has three tree components, each containing one root. The panels use different second states.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{7. disk}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The highlighted disk bounded by a five-edge cycle has $B=1$, $W=2$, $I=0$, $h=5$ and $l=1$. Thus $B+W=2I+h-2$, $B=I+l$ and $2l-h=-3$. The other two cycles of $D$ are suppressed.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{8. cancellation}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Reverse the dashed cycle by moving only the square marks. Blue disks, hence $s$ and its real weight, stay fixed. The phase changes from $i^3$ to $i$, so these two terms cancel. Applying the move twice restores the first panel.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{9. spokes}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Each directed edge moves through the centre of its own black triangle. Detours have disjoint interiors and exclude the third corner. With $a(t,v)=\tfrac  12\det (c_t,v)$, counterclockwise circulation is the positive area of the spoke polygon. The checked circulations are 5.641, 5.289, 11.033.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{10. white tree}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The dash-dot routes cross the deleted spokes $(t,s(t))$. They join all 12 white centres in one tree with 11 edges. Each route stays within the two white faces and their common black triangle. This is the cotree of the retained incidence tree.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{11. selected Jordan}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The bold black edges are $P$, one side of every face. The blue Jordan curve crosses precisely the unselected edges. Its two regions separate the two components of $P$; the exterior arcs represent edges incident with the outer-face dual vertex.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{12. recovered}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces The recovered partition includes the prescribed outer edge in one tree. The complementary dual cycle visits each of the 24 faces once and avoids the dual of that prescribed edge. This is the same cycle as in Figures 1 and 3.}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{13. gluing}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces This separate nine-vertex witness glues two octahedra along vertices $0,1,2$. The blue trees intersect in edge $01$; the orange trees intersect in vertex $2$. Exhaustive checking verifies both induced trees in the glued graph. Identify the boundary vertices rather than placing both interiors in the same planar disk.}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\tocsection {}{}{14. overview}}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Read down the main proof for a triangulation without separating triangles. The disk identity supports both the cancellation step and acyclicity of $P$. For a separating triangle, apply the construction to the capped pieces and glue using Figure 13. The flow records logical dependence, not an efficient algorithm.}}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
